{
  "sim_type": "excel",
  "title": "HDFC Bank & Kotak Mahindra: Comparing Margins & Drift",
  "scenario": "Your Mumbai equity desk is comparing two large-cap banks — HDFC Bank and Kotak Mahindra Bank — across FY20–FY24. Their absolute profit figures mislead (HDFC is larger); their common-size P&Ls reveal the real story. Build a five-year common-size analysis and compute CAGR to spot which bank is structurally strengthening.",
  "instructions": "Convert both banks' P&L data into common-size percentages (every line ÷ revenue), spot trends across years, and compute revenue CAGR to judge growth consistency.",
  "learning_objective": "Compute common-size P&L percentages across five years, identify margin drift and leverage trends, and calculate revenue CAGR to detect whether a bank's profitability structure is strengthening or deteriorating.",
  "hints": [
    "Every common-size cell divides the line by that year's revenue — the revenue row itself will always read 100%.",
    "For CAGR, count the years *between* FY20 and FY24 (that's 4 years, not 5 data points); formula is (End÷Start)^(1/4)−1.",
    "Drag your first common-size formula across columns and down rows — watch the percentages shift year by year to spot which line is drifting."
  ],
